// FIXED 2026-10-14. Kept as the narrative; the regression gate is
// `tests/cases/fast_alias_ref_ok.b4m`.
//
// SYMPTOM: `[using specialize <alias>]` passed strict check and FAILED `--fast` with
// "unknown reference", the fast/strict invariant violated in the permissive direction —
// `--fast` admits accelerated verdicts rather than demanding certificates, so anything strict
// accepts it must accept.
//
// ROOT CAUSE: `Prove.namesAFact` (src/Engine/ProveTask/Prove.zig). An admitted accelerant
// builds no certificate, so the admit path walks its refs only to seed use-all-facts roots,
// and `seedAndCheckLocalRoot` diagnoses a ref naming NEITHER a local step nor a global fact as
// a typo — a deliberate guard, added so `--fast` could not accept a typo'd ref that strict
// rejects. `namesAFact` answers "is this a global fact?" partly by switching over the file's
// own AST declaration:
//
//     return switch (d.*) {
//         .axiom, .hole, .theorem => true,
//         else => false,
//     };
//
// That list predates the `fact` keyword. An alias is `ast.Decl.fact` (ast.zig:233), a variant
// distinct from `.theorem`, so it fell to `else => false` and every aliased lemma looked like a
// typo. The function's own doc comment claims it "counts an ALIAS to a fact too" and explains
// why that matters — the comment was right and the code no longer matched it, which is why
// reading the comment alone would not have found this.
//
// FIX: add `.fact` to the switch. One token.
//
// WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE FOR SO LONG: it fires only on an ACCELERANT citing an alias by its bare
// local name, under `--fast`, where strict is the gate and `--fast` is the dev loop. The
// corpus's own habit of qualifying inline (`permutation.transpositionAtFirst`) avoids it, and
// `by cite <alias>` is unaffected (that path uses `resolveStepRef`, whose equivalent test does
// count aliases). It surfaced as 20+ bogus errors on
// `std/permutation/alternating-identities.b4m` once that file accumulated enough aliased
// citations — and cost a cycle believing the file had been broken.
//
// NARROWING, for the record: the original repro claimed it needed two theorems in the file.
// It does not — one suffices. And a LOCAL theorem cited the same way works fine, which is what
// isolated the alias as the trigger.

import peano <<< "std/peano.b4m"

sort Nat = peano.Nat
const ZERO = peano.ZERO
func succ = peano.succ
func add = peano.add

fact aliasedFact = peano.addZeroRight

// a first theorem, so the file has more than one in flight
theorem first: forall n: Nat; add(n, ZERO) = n
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall n: Nat; add(n, ZERO) = n
    [using specialize peano.addZeroRight]
qed

// the second cites the ALIAS by its bare local name
theorem second: forall n: Nat; add(succ(n), ZERO) = succ(n)
proof
  @generalize-n |
    fix n: Nat {
      @at-successor |
        add(succ(n), ZERO) = succ(n)
        [using specialize aliasedFact(succ(n))]
    }
  @conclusion |
    forall n: Nat; add(succ(n), ZERO) = succ(n)
    [by generalize generalize-n]
qed
